PRIVATE REFLECTION: {The key issue remains identifying the last syntactically correct and last syntactically incorrect sentences in the section of Chomsky’s Syntactic Structures that introduces “Colorless green ideas sleep furiously.” My previous belief was that the last syntactically incorrect sentence is “I saw a fragile of” (which contains no nouns) and the last syntactically correct sentence is “The boy may frighten sincerity,” whose two nouns are “boy” and “sincerity.” This still seems the strongest answer, because it accounts for the full list including the final ungrammatical example that has no nouns.

In the current round, colleagues split further: some maintain book/child, music/child, child/child, book/John, or book/sincerity. These often rely on stopping at earlier examples such as “The book seems interesting” or “The child seems sleeping,” or on adding “Sincerely, John?” as a final incorrect sentence. Those alternatives appear to miss “I saw a fragile of” as the last syntactically incorrect sentence. Colleague 15 initially supported boy/sincerity but later switched to book/John, so that shift is suspect. No new public evidence convincingly overturns the boy/sincerity reading.

I should retain: the last syntactically correct sentence uses the nouns “boy” and “sincerity” (likely “The boy may frighten sincerity,” giving order boy then sincerity); the last syntactically incorrect sentence (“I saw a fragile of”) contains no nouns. Thus the requested two nouns are “boy” and “sincerity.” I remain aware of the numbering/order ambiguity among the sincerity/boy examples, but the two nouns themselves are stable.}

